1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Some of the best moments of my life have happened in labs.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Also, some of the worst.

3
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Like, the time that I took a week to prepare a sample for NMR analysis

4
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:13,000
and then a classmate washed out the vials while I was sleeping for the first time in three days

5
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
because he couldn't find anything else to use for his experiment.

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Interestingly, that resulted in my personal most significant laboratory injury.

7
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:22,000
We all have one.

8
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
I cut my hand while punching a paper towel dispenser in frustration.

9
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
No one ever brought it up again.

10
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Alright, we're gonna talk about how to avoid injury in the lab

11
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,000
and some good techniques for using laboratory equipment correctly.

12
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000
First two lessons already learned, never wash out a vial you aren't familiar with

13
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:50,930
and don't punch paper towel dispensers.

14
00:00:50,930 --> 00:00:52,930
Let's start with some very basic safety stuff.

15
00:00:52,930 --> 00:00:53,930
Your hair.

16
00:00:53,930 --> 00:00:57,930
If your hair is long, it shall always be up in the lab.

17
00:00:57,930 --> 00:01:00,930
If it is not, it will catch on fire.

18
00:01:00,930 --> 00:01:01,930
I've seen it happen.

19
00:01:01,930 --> 00:01:06,930
It could also knock stuff over and include your vision and droop into your flask.

20
00:01:06,930 --> 00:01:08,930
Is there anything that might hang off your body in the lab?

21
00:01:08,930 --> 00:01:11,930
Droopy clothing, especially sleeves are total disaster.

22
00:01:11,930 --> 00:01:13,930
Clothes should cover your body as much as possible.

23
00:01:13,930 --> 00:01:14,930
I like to go long sleeve even.

24
00:01:14,930 --> 00:01:18,930
Never, ever, ever, ever, ever wear sandals in a lab.

25
00:01:18,930 --> 00:01:20,930
Long pants, closed-toed shoes and socks.

26
00:01:20,930 --> 00:01:22,930
Clothing that covers your entire torso.

27
00:01:22,930 --> 00:01:23,930
Always.

28
00:01:23,930 --> 00:01:26,930
I'm sure you look hunky in your muscle shirt

29
00:01:26,930 --> 00:01:30,930
and I have nothing against exposed midrips in principle, but not in the lab.

30
00:01:30,930 --> 00:01:33,930
Of course, your eyes are your most vulnerable organ.

31
00:01:33,930 --> 00:01:35,930
Always wear eye protection.

32
00:01:35,930 --> 00:01:37,930
And no, just glasses.

33
00:01:37,930 --> 00:01:38,930
Do not count.

34
00:01:38,930 --> 00:01:41,930
And if you feel like your eyes are starting to tingle or hurt,

35
00:01:41,930 --> 00:01:44,930
even if you don't know how something might have gotten in there,

36
00:01:44,930 --> 00:01:48,930
use the eye wash because it never hurts to be careful

37
00:01:48,930 --> 00:01:51,930
and, you know, we've all wondered what it feels like.

38
00:01:51,930 --> 00:01:54,930
I've actually never done this, so this will be a new experience for me.

39
00:01:59,590 --> 00:02:02,590
It does not feel particularly pleasant.

40
00:02:02,590 --> 00:02:04,590
But it's better than having your eyes burned out.

41
00:02:04,590 --> 00:02:05,590
Do not eat or drink in the lab.

42
00:02:05,590 --> 00:02:09,590
Despite our best efforts, stuff does sometimes get where we don't want it to be

43
00:02:09,590 --> 00:02:12,590
and if it gets in your food or you accidentally pick up the wrong cup,

44
00:02:12,590 --> 00:02:15,590
that's just a really embarrassing story for your obituary.

45
00:02:15,590 --> 00:02:18,590
Also, I would generally suggest avoiding working alone in a lab,

46
00:02:18,590 --> 00:02:21,590
especially if you're working with any machinery or hazardous substances,

47
00:02:21,590 --> 00:02:25,590
but it will be up to you and your advisors to make that decision when the time comes.

48
00:02:25,590 --> 00:02:27,590
You may have seen this little symbol before here.

49
00:02:27,590 --> 00:02:30,590
It's the hazardous materials or hazmat diamond

50
00:02:30,590 --> 00:02:32,590
and it's got some useful information in it.

51
00:02:32,590 --> 00:02:36,590
Each little box is ready to zero to four, zero being no big deal

52
00:02:36,590 --> 00:02:37,590
and four being...

53
00:02:37,590 --> 00:02:39,590
Holy monkey, be careful!

54
00:02:39,590 --> 00:02:42,590
Blue is for health, the red is for flammability

55
00:02:42,590 --> 00:02:44,590
and yellow is for chemical reactivity.

56
00:02:44,590 --> 00:02:48,590
A four in health means certain kinds of exposure will kill you.

57
00:02:48,590 --> 00:02:51,590
A four in fire is both very flammable and gaseous,

58
00:02:51,590 --> 00:02:53,590
so impossible to control outside of a closed container

59
00:02:53,590 --> 00:02:57,590
and a four in reactivity means that it is capable of exploding at room temperature.

60
00:02:57,590 --> 00:03:00,590
The little area underneath is for any extra information

61
00:03:00,590 --> 00:03:03,590
like if it's radioactive or reacts violently with water or something.

62
00:03:03,590 --> 00:03:05,590
If you ever need to know more about a chemical

63
00:03:05,590 --> 00:03:07,590
and what it might do to you or to the world,

64
00:03:07,590 --> 00:03:11,590
there's the good old MSDS, the Material Safety Data Sheet.

65
00:03:11,590 --> 00:03:15,590
Every chemical has one and it'll tell you all the terrible things that it might do to you.

66
00:03:15,590 --> 00:03:17,590
If you ever need to find one really fast,

67
00:03:17,590 --> 00:03:19,590
you don't need to go to the cabinet anymore,

68
00:03:19,590 --> 00:03:23,590
you can just google MSDS HCL or whatever chemical you think is in the process of killing your friend.

69
00:03:23,590 --> 00:03:26,590
There will be information on how best to treat the person who got exposed.

70
00:03:26,590 --> 00:03:32,590
Of course, you should have always read the MSDS before you even touch a chemical.

71
00:03:32,590 --> 00:03:37,590
Also, you should probably assume that every liquid in a lab that is not water is flammable.

72
00:03:37,590 --> 00:03:39,590
This baby here is a fume hood.

73
00:03:39,590 --> 00:03:44,590
It sucks all the air in there out so you don't have to breathe whatever's going on in there.

74
00:03:44,590 --> 00:03:48,590
It's also why it's impossible to keep chemistry labs at the proper temperature

75
00:03:48,590 --> 00:03:51,590
because the AC units and heating units are constantly pumping in controlled air,

76
00:03:51,590 --> 00:03:54,590
and these are constantly sucking it out.

77
00:03:54,590 --> 00:03:58,590
If you're doing some chemistry that might contain some noxious fumes, that goes on in here.

78
00:03:58,590 --> 00:04:02,590
And if you want it to work properly, first you've got to turn it on, that's the vent, that's the blower.

79
00:04:02,590 --> 00:04:04,590
Now it is sucking air.

80
00:04:04,590 --> 00:04:08,590
Second thing you want to do is make sure the sash is at the right level.

81
00:04:08,590 --> 00:04:11,590
This has a little thing that tells you where the sash is supposed to go.

82
00:04:11,590 --> 00:04:15,590
If you go above that on this model, it'll buzz at you.

83
00:04:15,590 --> 00:04:19,590
If the sash is higher than that, it's not going to properly vent all the stuff to the outside

84
00:04:19,590 --> 00:04:22,590
and some of it might get into your face, and that would be bad.

85
00:04:22,590 --> 00:04:27,590
A note here, if something happens to you in the lab, and if you don't know whether it was serious or minor, you're not sure,

86
00:04:27,590 --> 00:04:30,590
just tell your instructor, I once inhaled a bit of nitric oxide,

87
00:04:30,590 --> 00:04:34,590
which though initially extremely unpleasant seems to subside after a while,

88
00:04:34,590 --> 00:04:36,590
but it can have longer term effects.

89
00:04:36,590 --> 00:04:41,590
Headache, nausea, disorientation, dizziness, pulmonary edema, death.

90
00:04:41,590 --> 00:04:44,590
So I'm glad I fessed up so that I could get taken care of.

91
00:04:44,590 --> 00:04:48,590
Speaking of, if you want to know what something smells like, do not stick your face in it.

92
00:04:48,590 --> 00:04:50,590
Waft. Waft it toward your face.

93
00:04:50,590 --> 00:04:55,590
Also, never test something by tasting it, obviously, and never pipette by mouth.

94
00:04:55,590 --> 00:04:58,590
I hear people say that, and like, who would?

95
00:04:58,590 --> 00:05:01,590
But then I just found out that Heiko, the chemistry consultant for Crash Course,

96
00:05:01,590 --> 00:05:06,590
has twice gotten HCL in his mouth from pipetting by mouth, which I will never forgive you for.

97
00:05:06,590 --> 00:05:08,590
That is what these things are for.

98
00:05:08,590 --> 00:05:13,590
You put that on the end there, and then you go on, you draw the liquid up with this thing.

99
00:05:13,590 --> 00:05:14,590
That's what that's for.

100
00:05:14,590 --> 00:05:18,590
Also, these days, most pipetting is done by these guys, which are way cooler anyway.

101
00:05:18,590 --> 00:05:22,590
Pipetting is one way to move a substance from one container to another, and it's a pretty good one,

102
00:05:22,590 --> 00:05:26,590
but if you want to pour, you can pour, but let me just give you a tip.

103
00:05:26,590 --> 00:05:27,590
Commit.

104
00:05:27,590 --> 00:05:30,590
We tend to get all nervous when pouring stuff in the chemistry lab and go all slow with it,

105
00:05:30,590 --> 00:05:31,590
but that's terrible.

106
00:05:31,590 --> 00:05:34,590
You won't overcome the surface tension, and it'll dribble down the side of the container,

107
00:05:34,590 --> 00:05:37,590
so just commit at the beginning and at the end.

108
00:05:37,590 --> 00:05:39,590
Destroy that surface tension.

109
00:05:39,590 --> 00:05:40,590
Okay, back to safety.

110
00:05:40,590 --> 00:05:43,590
The most common lab injuries are cuts and punctures,

111
00:05:43,590 --> 00:05:46,590
and the most common source of those is cleaning up broken glass,

112
00:05:46,590 --> 00:05:50,590
which you should not do with your hands, but with a broom and a dustpan,

113
00:05:50,590 --> 00:05:54,590
and then deposit the results into a bin specifically for sharp stuff.

114
00:05:54,590 --> 00:05:58,590
But the worst thing, and it has happened so many times and it is so terrible,

115
00:05:58,590 --> 00:06:02,590
is forcing a glass rod or a thermometer or a piece of tubing through a stopper,

116
00:06:02,590 --> 00:06:07,590
and then it breaks, and then right into and through your hand,

117
00:06:07,590 --> 00:06:12,590
and you're in the hospital, and you've been paying for the rest of your life, probably.

118
00:06:12,590 --> 00:06:14,590
So when you're doing this, and it is sometimes necessary,

119
00:06:14,590 --> 00:06:18,590
you can use a bit of water or a lubricant or some kind of Vaseline

120
00:06:18,590 --> 00:06:21,590
to make it easier to go through, and then you hold it close

121
00:06:21,590 --> 00:06:23,590
and make sure your hand is not on the other side,

122
00:06:23,590 --> 00:06:27,590
so very close and make it go through like that.

123
00:06:27,590 --> 00:06:30,590
Not like this, because that's... no.

124
00:06:30,590 --> 00:06:34,590
When you're done with an experiment, do not just dump the results into the sink

125
00:06:34,590 --> 00:06:38,590
unless this has been explicitly approved by the person in charge of chemical safety.

126
00:06:38,590 --> 00:06:41,590
For some chemicals, like common acids or bases,

127
00:06:41,590 --> 00:06:44,590
dilution is the solution to pollution.

128
00:06:44,590 --> 00:06:47,590
When they get diluted, all that's left is common ions like chloride ions from HCl

129
00:06:47,590 --> 00:06:50,590
or sodium ions from sodium hydroxide,

130
00:06:50,590 --> 00:06:54,590
in addition to some protons or hydroxide ions that are neutralized by buffer ions

131
00:06:54,590 --> 00:06:56,590
that are present in your sewage system anyway.

132
00:06:56,590 --> 00:06:58,590
Bottom line is, they can be flushed with lots of water.

133
00:06:58,590 --> 00:07:01,590
For other chemicals, flushing is not a good idea.

134
00:07:01,590 --> 00:07:03,590
That probably won't hurt you, but it might hurt the environment.

135
00:07:03,590 --> 00:07:06,590
Do put the products into an appropriate waste container,

136
00:07:06,590 --> 00:07:08,590
but not just any waste container.

137
00:07:08,590 --> 00:07:11,590
Different solvents and reagents have to be disposed of differently,

138
00:07:11,590 --> 00:07:14,590
and if you dump some stuff into the wrong container,

139
00:07:14,590 --> 00:07:18,590
it could totally end up reacting with the other things that have been dumped in there.

140
00:07:18,590 --> 00:07:19,590
Not good.

141
00:07:19,590 --> 00:07:22,590
Rule of thumb, always know the right way to dispose of something

142
00:07:22,590 --> 00:07:24,590
before you even start to use it.

143
00:07:24,590 --> 00:07:27,590
This is an apron. It protects you and your clothes just a bit extra

144
00:07:27,590 --> 00:07:30,590
in case you're working with something hazardous, like concentrated acids.

145
00:07:30,590 --> 00:07:34,590
Aprons are nice because they're easy to get off if you spill something on them.

146
00:07:34,590 --> 00:07:38,590
Well, if you're wearing pants, you might hesitate a bit too long before you ditch them.

147
00:07:38,590 --> 00:07:43,590
Which reminds me, if you spill more than just a bit of anything super bad on your pants,

148
00:07:43,590 --> 00:07:47,590
modesty goes out the window. Just take them off.

149
00:07:47,590 --> 00:07:50,590
Also, while you're taking them off, you might want to run over to this guy here.

150
00:07:50,590 --> 00:07:54,590
His job is to dump a gigantic amount of water on you really fast.

151
00:07:54,590 --> 00:07:57,590
Now, usually, you don't get to see these things in action

152
00:07:57,590 --> 00:07:59,590
unless there's an actual emergency,

153
00:07:59,590 --> 00:08:04,590
but to thank you for sticking with us through this somewhat disjointed lecture on safety in the lab,

154
00:08:04,590 --> 00:08:06,590
I'm going to take this off.

155
00:08:06,590 --> 00:08:17,710
Thank you for watching this episode of Crash Course Chemistry.

156
00:08:17,710 --> 00:08:20,710
If you were listening, you learned what to wear in the lab,

157
00:08:20,710 --> 00:08:25,710
how to dispose of chemicals, how to avoid the most common accident in the laboratory,

158
00:08:25,710 --> 00:08:29,710
how to pour properly, what the hazmat diamond is, what an MSDS is,

159
00:08:29,710 --> 00:08:31,710
and how to use a fume hood.

